From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: damnbitimcrit <333091414+damnbitimcrit@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [APU] Fade audio gaps, prebuffer after them, catch up; thread
 priorities

When the game's audio falls behind (loads, shader compilation), the XAudio2
voice runs dry and plays silence until the next frame arrives, then
restarts at full level with one frame of margin, so it keeps running dry:
the output is chopped into hard-edged fragments, heard as a buzz or
screech.

- XAudio2 (drivers of AudioSystem clients, apu_prebuffer_frames > 0): when
  a processing pass reports bytes required, queue a fade out made of the
  last frame played backwards under a raised cosine (apu_fade_ms, 3 ms), so
  the output continues from its last sample down to zero. Then hold new
  frames until apu_prebuffer_frames (3) are there and fade the first in.
  Only XAudio2's report of a short pass counts as running dry, not an
  empty queue (the last frame can end exactly with a pass). The XAudio2
  thread only try-locks against SubmitFrame and skips the fade if a frame
  was submitted meanwhile; a short pass during a submit is only counted.
  Fade outs alternate between two buffers and don't release the client
  semaphore. The frequency ratio is set only when it changes. Media player
  drivers are unchanged.
- AudioSystem: the pump cadence never runs ahead, so every late pump
  lowered the output queue until it ran dry. After each pump, while the
  driver's queue is below apu_prebuffer_frames, request up to
  apu_max_catchup_frames (2) more frames at once, from a token per regular
  pump; the cadence is unchanged.
- apu_raise_thread_priority (default on): the XMA decoder thread runs at
  priority 24 like the Audio Worker (host highest), and guest threads based
  at priority 15 or higher (Halo 3: AUDIO, XAudio2's and the Waves
  library's threads) get the host's highest priority instead of normal; the
  guest-visible priority is unchanged.
- thread_priority_floor_normal (Kernel, default on): no thread runs below
  the host's normal priority. Xenia maps the guest priority 0 that Halo 3
  gives its RENDER, ASYNC_IO and NET_DEBUG threads to the host's lowest,
  where they starve behind shader compilation on every core while holding
  locks (the global critical region among them) that audio and drawing
  wait for. Before April 2026 Xenia ran every guest thread at normal.
- Counters (audio_stats.h, header only): regular and catch-up pumps, late
  and slow pumps with the slowest, runs dry with and without a fade,
  resumes, and the fewest frames queued; Halo 3's stats log them.
